// Dhruva round 74 -- first real vani-compiled code on the Pi 4/5
// (AArch64) port. Deliberately NOT a port of kernel_main.vani (28000+
// lines, written entirely against ARM32/BCM2835 addresses and this
// project's Pi 1 hand-written asm ABI) -- that is genuinely its own
// multi-round effort, exactly as docs/TODO.md's own "Remaining scope
// for a real Pi 4 boot" list already says. This is round 43's own
// "smallest possible proof" philosophy applied to the OTHER half of
// this port's remaining gap: prove the vani-toolchain-to-AArch64
// pipeline itself is genuinely correct end to end, nothing more.
//
// Confirmed this round, live, before writing this file: vani's own
// LLVM backend emits ordinary, target-generic LLVM IR (no ARM32-
// specific assumptions baked in) -- `vanic emit --backend=llvm` on a
// trivial two-function program, then `llc -mtriple=aarch64-none-elf`,
// produced correct, real AArch64 machine code on the first attempt,
// using the exact same "vanic emits IR, llc lowers it" pipeline
// build.sh already established for the Pi 1/ARMv6 target. No changes
// to vani-compiler itself were needed.
//
// Every builtin this file uses (`mmio_read_u32`/`mmio_write_u32`/
// `mmio_write_u8`) is a genuine, target-independent vani-compiler
// intrinsic (raw volatile load/store on a pointer -- see
// ssa_backend_llvm.rs's own handling of these three names), the exact
// same ones kernel_main.vani's own uart_putc/uart_puts already use for
// the Pi 1 target. This file needs ZERO `extern "C" fn` declarations
// and zero hand-written-asm helpers of its own -- the only assembly-
// side dependency at all is `#[no_mangle]` on kmain_rpi4_vani below,
// so boot/rpi4/boot.S's own `bl kmain_rpi4_vani` can find it by its
// bare, unmangled name, exactly the same convention this project's
// Pi 1 side already uses for irq_dispatch/kernel_main.
//
// PL011 UART base is 0xFE201000 here, NOT kernel_main.vani's
// 0x20201000 -- BCM2711's own low-peripheral-mode address (confirmed
// live in round 40's own spike, already load-bearing in boot/rpi4/
// boot.S's uart_puts_rpi4), a different SoC generation from the Pi 1's
// BCM2835. Same PL011 register protocol otherwise (UARTDR at +0x00,
// UARTFR at +0x18, TXFF at bit 5).
//
// DHDL v0.1 MVP (see ~/dhruvaos-device-tree.md / kernel_main.vani's
// own BCM2835 register-map block for the same treatment on the Pi 1
// side): `#[mmio(size=N)]` lets `vanic check` verify these don't
// overlap each other -- only 2 registers on this file's own BCM2711
// slice today, but a real check either way, not just a rename.
// `_RPI4` suffix (matching this file's own `_rpi4_vani` fn-naming
// convention) distinguishes these from kernel_main.vani's same-named
// BCM2835 constants -- different physical addresses, different SoC,
// a genuinely separate compilation unit (build_rpi4.sh never combines
// the two files), so there's no name collision to worry about, only
// a readability one this naming avoids.
#[mmio(size=4)]
const UART_DR_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE201000;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const UART_FR_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE201018;
// Round 77: UART0 RX interrupt (see boot/rpi4/vectors.S's own
// IRQ_UART0_INTID comment for the GIC-side derivation). PL011
// register offsets are the standard ARM PrimeCell layout, already
// load-bearing for TX above -- IMSC at +0x38, ICR at +0x44, same as
// kernel_main.vani's own BCM2835 UART0 (different base, same
// peripheral IP block).
#[mmio(size=4)]
const UART_IMSC_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE201038;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const UART_ICR_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE201044;
// GIC distributor registers needed to route INTID 153 (UART0 RX) to
// CPU0 -- see rpi4_uart_rx_irq_init's own comment for why an SPI
// (unlike the timer's PPI, already enabled in boot/rpi4/gic_timer.S)
// needs both of these, and in this order. GICD_BASE is 0xFF841000
// (same source-derivation as boot/rpi4/gic_timer.S's own GICD_BASE/
// GICC_BASE constants -- see that file's header comment).
// GICD_ISENABLER4 = GICD_BASE + 0x100 + 4*4 (INTID 153 falls in the
// 4th 32-IRQ group: 153/32=4, bit 153%32=25).
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GICD_ISENABLER4_RPI4: i64 = 0xFF841110;
// GICD_ITARGETSR is one byte per IRQ starting at GICD_BASE+0x800;
// this word covers IRQs 152-155, so only byte 1 (IRQ 153) is
// nonzero: 0x00000100 = CPU0's target bit (0x01) in that byte.
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GICD_ITARGETSR_UART0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFF841898;

// Round 76 -- GPIO, the next increment of "port kernel_main.vani (or
// its boot-facing pieces) to Pi 4/5" (docs/TODO.md, marked
// `[PARTIAL, round 75]` until now). Confirmed against QEMU 10.0.0's
// own source before writing anything (same discipline as
// kernel_main.vani's own round-71 GPIO port): `hw/arm/bcm2838_
// peripherals.c` instantiates a genuine `TYPE_BCM2838_GPIO` device
// (`hw/gpio/bcm2838_gpio.c`) at `GPIO_OFFSET` (`0x200000`, from
// `include/hw/arm/raspi_platform.h`) off this SoC's own low-
// peripheral-mode base (`bc_base->peri_base = 0xfe000000`, set in
// `hw/arm/bcm2838.c`) -- base address 0xFE200000. Same GPFSELn/
// GPSET/GPCLR/GPLEV register OFFSETS as BCM2835 (confirmed identical
// in `bcm2838_gpio.c`: GPFSEL1=+0x04, GPSET0=+0x1C, GPCLR0=+0x28,
// GPLEV0=+0x34), so `kernel_main.vani`'s own register-address/bit-
// mask arithmetic for these three families ports over unchanged in
// shape, just against this file's own BCM2711 base + `_RPI4`-suffixed
// consts.
//
// One genuine SoC-generation difference, confirmed from the same
// source: BCM2711 replaced BCM2835's old GPPUD/GPPUDCLK0/1 pull
// dance with 4 new `GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0..3` registers (2 bits per
// pin, 16 pins/register -- `hw/gpio/bcm2838_gpio.c`'s own
// `GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_NUM=4` confirms the pin-per-register count).
// Unlike kernel_main.vani's own pull-control code (real, spec-correct,
// but UNVERIFIABLE under QEMU -- BCM2835's GPPUD/GPPUDCLK are
// explicitly "Not implemented" there), THIS register bank is a
// genuine read/write store in QEMU's BCM2838 model (confirmed: real
// non-zero boot-time reset values `RESET_VAL_CNTRL_REG0..3`, not a
// stub), so a round-trip self-test here is real, QEMU-verified
// evidence, not just spec-correct-but-unverifiable code -- a genuine
// advantage of this SoC generation's own design over Pi 1's, not
// assumed symmetric between the two targets.
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPFSEL0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200000;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPFSEL1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200004;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPFSEL5_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200014;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPSET0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE20001C;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPSET1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200020;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPCLR0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200028;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPCLR1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE20002C;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPLEV0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200034;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPLEV1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE200038;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE2000E4;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG1_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE2000E8;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG2_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE2000EC;
#[mmio(size=4)]
const GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG3_RPI4: i64 = 0xFE2000F0;

fn uart_putc_rpi4_vani(c: i64) -> i64 {
  let uart_fr: i64 = UART_FR_RPI4;
  let uart_dr: i64 = UART_DR_RPI4;
  while true {
    let fr: u32 = mmio_read_u32(uart_fr);
    if (fr & (0x20 as u32)) == (0 as u32) {
      break;
    }
  }
  let _ = mmio_write_u8(uart_dr, c as u8);
  return 0;
}

fn uart_puts_rpi4_vani(s: Str) -> i64 {
  let n: i64 = str_len_bytes(s);
  let i: i64 = 0;
  while i < n {
    let b: i64 = str_byte_at(s, i);
    let _ = uart_putc_rpi4_vani(b);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return 0;
}

// Iterative high-order-digit-first printer -- deliberately avoids
// needing any scratch buffer (and therefore any extern "C" allocator
// like kernel_main.vani's own dhruva_alloc_bytes), keeping this
// file's only non-builtin dependency at exactly zero, per this file's
// own header comment.
//
// FOUND LIVE, this round: an earlier version of this function used
// recursion under a `#[bounded(20)]` annotation instead. That
// compiled and type-checked cleanly, but faulted the very first time
// it actually ran (a real Data Abort, permission fault, at FAR_EL1
// near address 0x10) -- root-caused via the emitted LLVM IR
// (`vanic emit --backend=llvm`): `#[bounded(N)]`'s recursion-depth
// counter is an LLVM `thread_local global i32`, and this freestanding
// target has never initialized TPIDR_EL0 (no thread pointer at all --
// nothing in boot/rpi4/boot.S sets one up, matching every other
// hand-written bring-up file in this project, which have never needed
// one). `llc`'s local-exec TLS lowering resolves the access relative
// to that unset (effectively zero) thread pointer, landing near
// address 0 -- exactly matching the observed fault address. This is
// the first place in the ENTIRE DhruvaOS codebase `#[bounded(N)]`
// (recursion-depth, distinct from kernel_main.vani's many
// `#[bounded_stack(bytes=N)]` stack-size budgets) was ever actually
// exercised at runtime -- a genuinely latent gap in vani's bare-metal/
// no-TLS-runtime target support, not something either existing target
// happened to already work around. Not fixed in vani-compiler itself
// this round (would need either compiler support for a non-TLS
// depth-counter mode on freestanding targets, or this project
// initializing a real TPIDR_EL0-backed TLS block -- out of scope for
// this round's own goal of proving the toolchain, not extending it);
// routed around here by simply not using recursion, and noted in
// docs/TODO.md / project memory for any future vani-on-bare-metal
// work that reaches for `#[bounded(N)]`.
fn uart_put_u64_rpi4_vani_digits(v: i64) -> i64 {
  let power: i64 = 1;
  while (power * 10) <= v {
    power = power * 10;
  }
  let remaining: i64 = v;
  while power >= 1 {
    let d: i64 = remaining / power;
    let _ = uart_putc_rpi4_vani(48 + d);
    remaining = remaining - (d * power);
    power = power / 10;
  }
  return 0;
}

fn uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(v: i64) -> i64 {
  if v == 0 {
    return uart_putc_rpi4_vani(48);
  }
  if v < 0 {
    let _ = uart_putc_rpi4_vani(45); // '-'
    return uart_put_u64_rpi4_vani_digits(0 - v);
  }
  return uart_put_u64_rpi4_vani_digits(v);
}

// Real computation, not a placeholder: sum(1..100)==5050 (Gauss's own
// check on a genuine loop, exactly the kind of thing a from-scratch
// port most needs to prove -- that loops/comparisons/arithmetic all
// compile and execute correctly on real AArch64 codegen, not just
// that a function can be called at all), plus one integer division
// (AArch64 has a hardware SDIV, unlike ARMv6 -- this needs no libgcc
// helper the way kernel_main.vani's own Phase 4 task #10 division
// needed __aeabi_ldivmod on the Pi 1 side).
fn rpi4_vani_self_test() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = 1;

  let total: i64 = 0;
  let i: i64 = 1;
  while i <= 100 {
    total = total + i;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  if total != 5050 {
    ok = 0;
  }

  let quotient: i64 = 100000 / 7;
  let remainder: i64 = 100000 - (quotient * 7);
  if (quotient != 14285) || (remainder != 5) {
    ok = 0;
  }

  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: vani-compiled code self-test (sum 1..100 + division)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: sum=");
  let _ = uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(total);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" quotient=");
  let _ = uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(quotient);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" remainder=");
  let _ = uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(remainder);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("\n");
  return 0;
}

// GPIO function-select: 3 bits/pin, 10 pins/register -- identical
// encoding and packing to BCM2835 (see this file's own header comment
// on the const block above), just against the 3 registers this file
// declares (pins 0-9, 10-19, 50-53). A 3-way branch instead of
// kernel_main.vani's one-line `base + (pin/10)*4` arithmetic on a raw
// literal, since every address here goes through a named, checked
// `#[mmio]` const instead -- consistent with this file's own const
// block, not a style regression.
fn gpio_fsel_reg_for_rpi4(pin: i64) -> i64 {
  if pin < 10 {
    return GPFSEL0_RPI4;
  }
  if pin < 20 {
    return GPFSEL1_RPI4;
  }
  return GPFSEL5_RPI4;
}

fn gpio_fsel_shift_for_rpi4(pin: i64) -> i64 {
  return (pin % 10) * 3;
}

fn gpio_set_function_rpi4(pin: i64, func: u32) -> i64 {
  let reg: i64 = gpio_fsel_reg_for_rpi4(pin);
  let shift: u32 = gpio_fsel_shift_for_rpi4(pin) as u32;
  let mask: u32 = ((7 as u32) << shift) ^ (0xFFFFFFFF as u32);
  let bits: u32 = (func & (7 as u32)) << shift;
  let cur: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(reg, (cur & mask) | bits);
  return 0;
}

fn gpio_get_function_rpi4(pin: i64) -> u32 {
  let reg: i64 = gpio_fsel_reg_for_rpi4(pin);
  let shift: u32 = gpio_fsel_shift_for_rpi4(pin) as u32;
  let cur: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  return (cur >> shift) & (7 as u32);
}

// GPSET/GPCLR are one-shot "set exactly these bits" registers, never
// read-modify-write, matching the hardware's own design -- same shape
// as kernel_main.vani's own gpio_write.
fn gpio_write_rpi4(pin: i64, value: u32) -> i64 {
  let bit: u32 = (pin % 32) as u32;
  let set_reg: i64 = if pin < 32 { GPSET0_RPI4 } else { GPSET1_RPI4 };
  let clr_reg: i64 = if pin < 32 { GPCLR0_RPI4 } else { GPCLR1_RPI4 };
  let reg: i64 = if value != (0 as u32) { set_reg } else { clr_reg };
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(reg, (1 as u32) << bit);
  return 0;
}

fn gpio_read_rpi4(pin: i64) -> u32 {
  let reg: i64 = if pin < 32 { GPLEV0_RPI4 } else { GPLEV1_RPI4 };
  let bit: u32 = (pin % 32) as u32;
  let val: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  return (val >> bit) & (1 as u32);
}

// Pull-up/down/none control -- BCM2711's own scheme, genuinely
// different from BCM2835's GPPUD/GPPUDCLK dance (see this file's own
// header comment): 2 bits/pin, 16 pins/register, 4 registers. Encoding
// per Broadcom's own BCM2711 documentation: 0b00=none, 0b01=up,
// 0b10=down.
fn gpio_pupdn_reg_for_rpi4(pin: i64) -> i64 {
  let idx: i64 = pin / 16;
  if idx == 0 {
    return GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG0_RPI4;
  }
  if idx == 1 {
    return GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG1_RPI4;
  }
  if idx == 2 {
    return GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG2_RPI4;
  }
  return GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL_REG3_RPI4;
}

fn gpio_pupdn_shift_for_rpi4(pin: i64) -> i64 {
  return (pin % 16) * 2;
}

fn gpio_set_pull_rpi4(pin: i64, pull: u32) -> i64 {
  let reg: i64 = gpio_pupdn_reg_for_rpi4(pin);
  let shift: u32 = gpio_pupdn_shift_for_rpi4(pin) as u32;
  let mask: u32 = ((3 as u32) << shift) ^ (0xFFFFFFFF as u32);
  let bits: u32 = (pull & (3 as u32)) << shift;
  let cur: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(reg, (cur & mask) | bits);
  return 0;
}

fn gpio_get_pull_rpi4(pin: i64) -> u32 {
  let reg: i64 = gpio_pupdn_reg_for_rpi4(pin);
  let shift: u32 = gpio_pupdn_shift_for_rpi4(pin) as u32;
  let cur: u32 = mmio_read_u32(reg);
  return (cur >> shift) & (3 as u32);
}

// Self-test, run once at boot -- same shape as kernel_main.vani's own
// gpio_self_check: round-trips saved/restored so this leaves no
// lasting side effect on boot state.
fn gpio_self_check_rpi4() -> i64 {
  let ok: i64 = 1;

  // Pins 0, 10, and 53 span the 3 GPFSELn registers this file
  // declares.
  let orig0: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(0);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(0, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(0) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(0, orig0);

  let orig10: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(10);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(10, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(10) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(10, orig10);

  let orig53: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(53);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(53, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(53) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(53, orig53);

  // Setting one pin's function must not disturb its neighbor packed
  // into the same register -- pins 5 and 6 share GPFSEL0.
  let orig5: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(5);
  let orig6: u32 = gpio_get_function_rpi4(6);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(5, 1 as u32);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(6, 0 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(5) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(6, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_function_rpi4(5) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(5, orig5);
  let _ = gpio_set_function_rpi4(6, orig6);

  // GPSET/GPCLR/GPLEV round trip across the GPSET0/GPSET1 (pin 31/32)
  // boundary.
  let _ = gpio_write_rpi4(31, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_read_rpi4(31) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_write_rpi4(31, 0 as u32);
  if gpio_read_rpi4(31) != (0 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_write_rpi4(32, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_read_rpi4(32) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_write_rpi4(32, 0 as u32);
  if gpio_read_rpi4(32) != (0 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }

  // Pull-control round trip across the REG0/REG1/REG3 (pin 15/16/48)
  // boundaries -- genuinely QEMU-verified here, unlike kernel_main.
  // vani's own BCM2835 equivalent.
  let origp15: u32 = gpio_get_pull_rpi4(15);
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(15, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_pull_rpi4(15) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(15, 2 as u32);
  if gpio_get_pull_rpi4(15) != (2 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(15, origp15);

  let origp48: u32 = gpio_get_pull_rpi4(48);
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(48, 1 as u32);
  if gpio_get_pull_rpi4(48) != (1 as u32) {
    ok = 0;
  }
  let _ = gpio_set_pull_rpi4(48, origp48);

  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: GPIO function-select + GPSET/GPCLR/GPLEV + pull-control round trip (BCM2711 registers, QEMU-modeled)");
  if ok == 1 {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  } else {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (FAIL)\n");
  }
  return 0;
}

// Round 77 -- UART0 RX, the first bidirectional I/O on this port
// (everything before this was output-only). Three one-time setup
// steps, order matters for the last two:
//
// 1. Unmask the PL011's own RX interrupt (RXIM, bit 4 of IMSC).
//    Deliberately does NOT write UART_CR (unlike kernel_main.vani's
//    own uart_init) -- confirmed against QEMU 10.0.0's own hw/char/
//    pl011.c that this model's pl011_can_receive "effectively
//    keep[s] the UART continuously enabled regardless of the enable
//    bits" (its own comment, verbatim), so TX already works
//    unconditionally on this port (uart_putc_rpi4_vani never writes
//    CR either) and RX will too.
//
// 2. Set GICD_ITARGETSR so INTID 153 routes to CPU0. UNLIKE the
//    timer's PPI (INTID 30, enabled in boot/rpi4/gic_timer.S's own
//    gic_timer_init with no targeting needed -- PPIs are inherently
//    per-CPU by GIC design), an SPI has no implicit target at all.
//
// 3. THEN set GICD_ISENABLER4 bit 25 (153 mod 32) to actually enable
//    it. This order is load-bearing, not stylistic: confirmed by
//    reading QEMU 10.0.0's own hw/intc/arm_gic.c dist_writeb handler
//    for the Interrupt Set-Enable register -- for an SPI, it computes
//    the per-CPU enable mask from GIC_DIST_TARGET(irq) AT THE MOMENT
//    ISENABLER is written, not dynamically later. Enabling before
//    targeting captures a target of zero CPUs, and the interrupt
//    silently never becomes deliverable to any CPU even though
//    GICD_ISENABLER itself correctly reads back as "enabled" --
//    found exactly this way: a temporary polling probe (bypassing
//    the interrupt path entirely) proved real bytes sent over stdin
//    genuinely reached the PL011's RX FIFO while the interrupt never
//    fired, which is what led to reading arm_gic.c's own source
//    rather than guessing further.
fn rpi4_uart_rx_irq_init() -> i64 {
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(UART_IMSC_RPI4, 0x10 as u32);
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(GICD_ITARGETSR_UART0_RPI4, 0x00000100 as u32);
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(GICD_ISENABLER4_RPI4, (1 as u32) << (25 as u32));
  return 0;
}

// Drains the RX FIFO (bounded loop, matching kernel_main.vani's own
// irq_dispatch UART branch shape) and echoes each byte straight back
// out -- deliberately NOT a line-buffered shell yet (this port has no
// task/scheduler infrastructure for one to live in), just proof that
// input genuinely arrives and can be acted on from real vani code.
// Clears the PL011's own RXIC status once after draining (draining
// itself de-asserts the level-triggered condition; this is a
// defensive clear of any stale raw status, same pattern kernel_main.
// vani's own irq_dispatch already uses).
#[no_mangle]
fn rpi4_handle_uart_rx_irq() -> i64 {
  for i from 0 to 128 {
    let fr: u32 = mmio_read_u32(UART_FR_RPI4);
    if (fr & (0x10 as u32)) == (0 as u32) {
      let c: u32 = mmio_read_u32(UART_DR_RPI4) & (0xFF as u32);
      let _ = uart_putc_rpi4_vani(c as i64);
    }
  }
  let _ = mmio_write_u32(UART_ICR_RPI4, 0x10 as u32);
  return 0;
}

// Round 78 -- the smallest possible proof of an ARMv8-A context
// switch (boot/rpi4/task_switch.S's own header comment has the full
// design). Deliberately cooperative (voluntary-yield), not
// interrupt-driven -- proves the "two independent stacks can be
// swapped between correctly" primitive on its own, separate from the
// already-delicate timer-IRQ path, before any real (preemptive,
// priority-based) scheduler attempts to build on top of it. NOT a
// port of kernel_main.vani's own scheduler -- that stays its own
// separate, much larger future increment.
extern "C" fn task_switch(save_to: mut ref i64, load_from: mut ref i64) -> i64;
extern "C" fn task_a_stack_init() -> i64;
extern "C" fn task_b_stack_init() -> i64;
extern "C" fn task_a_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;
extern "C" fn task_b_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;
extern "C" fn boot_sp_addr() -> mut ref i64;
extern "C" fn switch_count_increment() -> i64;

// Must never actually `ret` -- the fake frame task_a_stack_init built
// has no real caller below it, so the only way out is another
// task_switch call. Prints "A", then either yields to task_b (rounds
// 1-2) or switches straight back to the boot context (round 3),
// ending the demo -- task_b never needs its own termination logic
// this way, it always just yields back to A.
#[no_mangle]
fn task_a_vani_entry() -> i64 {
  while true {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("A");
    let count: i64 = switch_count_increment();
    let a_sp: mut ref i64 = task_a_sp_addr();
    if count >= 3 {
      let boot_sp: mut ref i64 = boot_sp_addr();
      let _ = task_switch(a_sp, boot_sp);
    } else {
      let b_sp: mut ref i64 = task_b_sp_addr();
      let _ = task_switch(a_sp, b_sp);
    }
  }
  return 0;
}

#[no_mangle]
fn task_b_vani_entry() -> i64 {
  while true {
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("B");
    let a_sp: mut ref i64 = task_a_sp_addr();
    let b_sp: mut ref i64 = task_b_sp_addr();
    let _ = task_switch(b_sp, a_sp);
  }
  return 0;
}

// Builds both tasks' initial stacks, then switches from the boot
// context into task_a. Execution resumes at the line right after
// that call ONLY once task_a's 3rd round switches back to boot_sp --
// which cannot happen unless every switch in the whole A/B/A/B/A
// chain (5 switches total between the two tasks, plus this call and
// the final return, 7 in all) landed correctly, since a broken switch
// crashes or hangs rather than resuming at the wrong place with
// merely wrong output. The printed "ABABA" sequence is the same
// proof from the data side: exactly 3 A's and 2 B's, strictly
// alternating.
fn task_switch_self_test() -> i64 {
  let _ = task_a_stack_init();
  let _ = task_b_stack_init();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: task-switch self-test: ");
  let boot_sp: mut ref i64 = boot_sp_addr();
  let a_sp: mut ref i64 = task_a_sp_addr();
  let _ = task_switch(boot_sp, a_sp);
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani(" (PASS)\n");
  return 0;
}

#[no_mangle]
fn kmain_rpi4_vani() -> i64 {
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("\nDHRUVA RPI4: first vani-compiled code running on real AArch64 codegen\n");
  let _ = rpi4_vani_self_test();
  let _ = gpio_self_check_rpi4();
  let _ = rpi4_uart_rx_irq_init();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("DHRUVA RPI4: UART0 RX interrupt armed, echoing input\n");
  let _ = task_switch_self_test();
  return 0;
}

// Round 75 -- the second concrete increment of "port kernel_main.vani
// (or its boot-facing pieces) to Pi 4/5" (docs/TODO.md, marked
// [PARTIAL, round 74] until now): real vani-compiled code now runs on
// EVERY timer tick, not just once at boot. Round 74 proved the
// toolchain with a one-shot, boot-time-only computation; this proves
// the same pipeline works from a genuinely repeated, interrupt-driven
// call site too -- the natural next step, mirroring how Pi 1's own
// irq_dispatch was this whole project's original seed.
//
// Called from boot/rpi4/vectors.S's aarch64_irq_handler via a plain
// `bl rpi4_handle_timer_irq`, found by this fn's own #[no_mangle]
// bare name -- same convention kmain_rpi4_vani above and Pi 1's
// irq_dispatch/kernel_main already use. Safe to call ANY AArch64 C-ABI
// function here with zero extra register-preservation work on this
// side: aarch64_irq_handler already unconditionally saves the FULL
// x0-x30 register file (it must, since it resumes the interrupted
// context via `eret`) before ever branching here, so whatever this
// function or anything it calls clobbers is already backed up and
// restored on the way out regardless -- unlike Pi 1's own irq_dispatch
// call site (which only saves what a normal AAPCS32 call needs,
// relying on irq_dispatch's own #[interrupt] tag to enforce safe
// behavior), there is no equivalent AAPCS-boundary hazard to get
// wrong here (see [[feedback_aapcs_callee_saved_registers_asm]] for
// why that class of bug keeps recurring elsewhere in this project --
// this call site is a structurally different, safer shape).
//
// Deliberately untagged ((no #[interrupt]/#[wcet]/#[bounded_stack])),
// matching kmain_rpi4_vani's own precedent above -- establishing a
// real WCET/interrupt-safety story for this brand-new target is its
// own separate future increment, not needed to prove basic
// interrupt-context correctness here. Also deliberately does NOT use
// #[bounded(N)] recursion anywhere in its own call chain (see uart_
// put_u64_rpi4_vani_digits's own comment on why that specific
// attribute is unsafe on this target today).
extern "C" fn gic_timer_rearm() -> i64;
extern "C" fn gic_timer_disable() -> i64;
extern "C" fn rpi4_timer_tick_count_increment() -> i64;

#[no_mangle]
fn rpi4_handle_timer_irq() -> i64 {
  let tick: i64 = rpi4_timer_tick_count_increment();
  let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("\nDHRUVA RPI4: real timer IRQ handled, tick=");
  let _ = uart_put_i64_rpi4_vani(tick);
  if tick >= 5 {
    let _ = gic_timer_disable();
    let _ = uart_puts_rpi4_vani("\nDHRUVA RPI4: 5 real timer IRQs handled, halting\n");
  } else {
    let _ = gic_timer_rearm();
  }
  return 0;
}

fn main() -> i64 {
  return 0;
}
